 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of how lookup is served. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookup is served from a local embedded SQLite index file on the kiosk. No other component sees the index, its file path, or its query language.
+
+## Local Index
+
+Each kiosk holds one SQLite database file per imported snapshot, keyed by normalized serial number, carrying the snapshot date and schema version alongside the records. The kiosk builds this file itself from the signed snapshot's records; the index is derived data and is never signed or copied between kiosks. The active snapshot is the one database file the registry currently points to. Lookup is a single keyed read against that file.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the new SQLite index in a separate staging file, never in the active one, so readers keep using the previous snapshot until activation. Activate by atomically switching the active-snapshot pointer to the staged file only after the signature, schema, uniqueness, and index checks have all succeeded. An import failure discards the staging file, leaves the previous snapshot active, and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. Lookup is an exact-match read on the normalized key in the active SQLite index.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index built on the kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Lookups must complete from files on the kiosk with no runtime network access, which a networked directory or hosted search cannot meet. SQLite gives predictable keyed lookups with no service to run; the cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its index when a snapshot arrives, which stays inside this node. |
+| Index provenance | Rebuild on each kiosk from the signed records | Ship a prebuilt index in the snapshot | The signed record stream stays the only trusted input, and the snapshot format stays independent of the SQLite file format. Costs import time on each kiosk. |
+| Activation | Build in a staging file, then atomically switch the active pointer | Import in place inside one transaction | An interrupted or failed import cannot touch the file readers use, so the previous snapshot stays available. Costs disk space for two index files during import. |
